https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-11-nibiru/blob/main/x/evm/keeper/msg_server.go#L249


# Vulnerability details

## Finding description and impact

At the moment `ApplyEvmMsg()` does not account for coinbase fees breaking compatibility with the standard EVM execution client. There is always a priority fee parameter that reflects how much fees has to be paid to the validator of the block but it's currently missing.

## Proof of Concept

Take a look at the geth client implementation:

https://github.com/NibiruChain/go-ethereum/blob/nibiru/geth/core/state_transition.go#L351-359
```
if st.evm.Config.NoBaseFee && st.gasFeeCap.Sign() == 0 && st.gasTipCap.Sign() == 0 {
		// Skip fee payment when NoBaseFee is set and the fee fields
		// are 0. This avoids a negative effectiveTip being applied to
		// the coinbase when simulating calls.
	} else {
		fee := new(big.Int).SetUint64(st.gasUsed())
		fee.Mul(fee, effectiveTip)
		st.state.AddBalance(st.evm.Context.Coinbase, fee)
	}

```

When sending a transaction, the Nibiru `backend` module includes `MaxPriorityFeePerGas` parameter even if it's not set by a user:

https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-11-nibiru/blob/main/eth/rpc/backend/call_tx.go#L103-109
```
if args.MaxPriorityFeePerGas == nil {
				tip, err := b.SuggestGasTipCap(head.BaseFee)
				if err != nil {
					return args, err
				}
				args.MaxPriorityFeePerGas = (*hexutil.Big)(tip)
			}
```


But when applying a message, there is no transfer to the coinbase address at all:

https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-11-nibiru/blob/main/x/evm/keeper/msg_server.go#L249

The function only calculates the gas and subtracts all the gas costs from the gas limit but there is no any transfer of priority fee to the coinbase address.

## Recommended mitigation steps

Add coinbase address transfer similar to the vanilla geth client implementation.

# Lines of code

https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-11-nibiru/blob/main/x/evm/keeper/msg_server.go#L594


# Vulnerability details

## Finding description and impact

Currently `msg_server` in the `keeper` module allows users to convert tokens that were initially ERC20 tokens (now coins) back to the ERC20 standard tokens. However, the logic is flawed as there is no way to verify that the contract holds the necessary amount of tokens and it also burns the coins which is an incorrect way to do it resulting in a loss for protocol.

## Proof of Concept

Take a look at the implementation of the `convertCoinToEvmBornERC20()`:

https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-11-nibiru/blob/main/x/evm/keeper/msg_server.go#L575-613
```
// 1 | Caller transfers Bank Coins to be converted to ERC20 tokens.
	if err := k.Bank.SendCoinsFromAccountToModule(
		ctx,
		sender,
		evm.ModuleName,
		sdk.NewCoins(coin),
	); err != nil {
		return nil, errors.Wrap(err, "error sending Bank Coins to the EVM")
	}

	// 2 | EVM sends ERC20 tokens to the "to" account.
	// This should never fail due to the EVM account lacking ERc20 fund because
	// the an account must have sent the EVM module ERC20 tokens in the mapping
	// in order to create the coins originally.
	//
	// Said another way, if an asset is created as an ERC20 and some amount is
	// converted to its Bank Coin representation, a balance of the ERC20 is left
	// inside the EVM module account in order to convert the coins back to
	// ERC20s.
	actualSentAmount, _, err := k.ERC20().Transfer(
		erc20Addr,
		evm.EVM_MODULE_ADDRESS,
		recipient,
		coin.Amount.BigInt(),
		ctx,
	)
	if err != nil {
		return nil, errors.Wrap(err, "failed to transfer ERC-20 tokens")
	}

	// 3 | In the FunToken ERC20 → BC conversion process that preceded this
	// TxMsg, the Bank Coins were minted. Consequently, to preserve an invariant
	// on the sum of the FunToken's bank and ERC20 supply, we burn the coins here
	// in the BC → ERC20 conversion.
	burnCoin := sdk.NewCoin(coin.Denom, sdk.NewIntFromBigInt(actualSentAmount))
	err = k.Bank.BurnCoins(ctx, evm.ModuleName, sdk.NewCoins(burnCoin))
	if err != nil {
		return nil, errors.Wrap(err, "failed to burn coins")
	}


```

According to the description:


```
// Converts a coin that was originally an ERC20 token, and that was converted to
// a bank coin, back to an ERC20 token. EVM module does not own the ERC-20
// contract and cannot mint the ERC-20 tokens. EVM module has escrowed tokens in
// the first conversion from ERC-20 to bank coin.
```

A user first sends tokens to the EVM module. Then the ERC20 tokens are transferred to the user and then coins are burnt. The problem is that it's not checked if such amount of tokens has been escrowed before. So if a user wants to convert 100 tokens back to the ERC20, there is no way to ensure that ERC20 contract holds such amount of tokens:

https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-11-nibiru/blob/main/x/evm/keeper/msg_server.go#L594-600
```
actualSentAmount, _, err := k.ERC20().Transfer(
		erc20Addr,
		evm.EVM_MODULE_ADDRESS,
		recipient,
		coin.Amount.BigInt(),
		ctx,
	)


```

So this transfer can basically fail if there is no sufficient amount of tokens that lie in the ERC20 contract. Moreover, the coins are burnt afterwards:

https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-11-nibiru/blob/main/x/evm/keeper/msg_server.go#L610
```
err = k.Bank.BurnCoins(ctx, evm.ModuleName, sdk.NewCoins(burnCoin))
```

So the protocol transfers some amount of tokens and then burns the coins leading to a loss for protocol. Moreover, there is no any functionality implemented to escrow the tokens - according to the comments, a user had to first perform conversion from ERC20 token to the bank coin and then when it converts it back, the contract has enough liquidity to do so. However, currently, funToken can only be created and converted from bank coin to ERC20 token.

## Recommended mitigation steps

Lock the tokens instead of burning them after transfer of ERC20 tokens has been performed.

# Lines of code

https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-11-nibiru/blob/main/x/evm/keeper/msg_server.go#L520
https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-11-nibiru/blob/main/x/evm/keeper/msg_server.go#L567


# Vulnerability details

## Finding description and impact

Currently there are 2 way to transfer liquidity between ERC20 tokens and Bank coins such as Nibiru:

1. `convertCoinToEvmBornERC20()` - the function converts a coin that was originally an ERC20 token, and that was converted to a bank coin, back to an ERC20 token.

2. `convertCoinToEvmBornCoin()` - the function Converts Bank Coins for FunToken mapping that was born from a coin (IsMadeFromCoin=true) into the ERC20 tokens.


The problem is that in both functions there is no any check to ensure that the coin was originally an ERC20 token or not because they only fetch the ERC20 address from the FunToken mapping.

## Proof of Concept

https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-11-nibiru/blob/main/x/evm/keeper/msg_server.go#L567-583
```
func (k Keeper) convertCoinToEvmBornERC20(
	ctx sdk.Context,
	sender sdk.AccAddress,
	recipient gethcommon.Address,
	coin sdk.Coin,
	funTokenMapping evm.FunToken,
) (*evm.MsgConvertCoinToEvmResponse, error) {
	erc20Addr := funTokenMapping.Erc20Addr.Address
	// 1 | Caller transfers Bank Coins to be converted to ERC20 tokens.
	if err := k.Bank.SendCoinsFromAccountToModule(
		ctx,
		sender,
		evm.ModuleName,
		sdk.NewCoins(coin),
	); err != nil {
		return nil, errors.Wrap(err, "error sending Bank Coins to the EVM")
	}


```

https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-11-nibiru/blob/main/x/evm/keeper/msg_server.go#L527-534
```
// 1 | Send Bank Coins to the EVM module
	err := k.Bank.SendCoinsFromAccountToModule(ctx, sender, evm.ModuleName, sdk.NewCoins(coin))
	if err != nil {
		return nil, errors.Wrap(err, "failed to send coins to module account")
	}

	// 2 | Mint ERC20 tokens to the recipient
	erc20Addr := funTokenMapping.Erc20Addr.Address

```

As you can see here, both methods just fetch `funTokenMapping.Erc20Addr.Address` (not checking `isMadeFromCoin` parameter) and then proceed with the implementation. However, the behavior is not correct and allows users to use methods for unintended operations such as unintended convers (for example, performing minting instead of transfer and so on).

## Recommended mitigation steps

Check `isMadeFromCoin` parameter first and then proceed with the corresponding implementation.

# Lines of code

https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-11-nibiru/blob/8ed91a036f664b421182e183f19f6cef1a4e28ea/app/evmante/evmante_validate_basic.go#L125-L130


# Vulnerability details


### Proof of Concept

In `app/evmante/evmante_validate_basic.go`, fees are constructed without proper validation:

https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-11-nibiru/blob/8ed91a036f664b421182e183f19f6cef1a4e28ea/app/evmante/evmante_validate_basic.go#L125-L130

```go
		txFee = txFee.Add(
			sdk.Coin{
				Denom:  evm.EVMBankDenom,
				Amount: sdkmath.NewIntFromBigInt(txData.Fee()),
			},
		)
```

Note that the `txfee` is `sdk.Coins` and not because it is a list of coins, see:
https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-11-nibiru/blob/8ed91a036f664b421182e183f19f6cef1a4e28ea/app/evmante/evmante_validate_basic.go#L89

```go
	txFee := sdk.Coins{}
```

Now querying `Add()` with a `coin` on this list is crucial and needs to be carefully done, see the cosmos sdk documentation of `Add`:

```go
// Add adds two sets of coins.
//
// e.g.
// {2A} + {A, 2B} = {3A, 2B}
// {2A} + {0B} = {2A}
//
// NOTE: Add operates under the invariant that coins are sorted by
// denominations.
//
// CONTRACT: Add will never return Coins where one Coin has a non-positive
// amount. In otherwords, IsValid will always return true.
// The function panics if `coins` or  `coinsB` are not sorted (ascending).
func (coins Coins) Add(coinsB ...Coin) Coins {
	return coins.safeAdd(coinsB)
}

```

Now we can see that this function would never return coins if one has a non-positive amount, which is why the Cosmos SDK provides `NewCoin` which includes important validations that ensures that the amount is not negative and that the denomination is valid which Ethermint use when adding the fee to the transaction _(which Nibiru's implementation is based on)_, see [https://github.com/kshlsa/ethermint/blob/v0.15.0/app/ante/eth.go#L435](https://github.com/kshlsa/ethermint/blob/v0.15.0/app/ante/eth.go#L435):

Below is the `NewCoin` function from the Cosmos SDK:

```go
// Coin

// NewCoin returns a new coin with a denomination and amount. It will panic if
// the amount is negative or if the denomination is invalid.
func NewCoin(denom string, amount Int) Coin {
    coin := Coin{
        Denom:  denom,
        Amount: amount,
    }

    if err := coin.Validate(); err != nil {
        panic(err)
    }

    return coin
}

func (coin Coin) Validate() error {
    if err := ValidateDenom(denom); err != nil {
        return err
    }

    if coin.Amount.IsNegative() {
        return fmt.Errorf("negative coin amount: %v", coin.Amount)
    }

    return nil
}
```

These validations ensure that the following conditions are met:

1. Valid denomination format
2. Non-negative amounts
3. Proper coin structure

### Impact

Borderline, considering the direct construction of `sdk.Coin` without proper validation could lead to not only invalid fee calculations and potential panics in production, but also the fact that the `Add` function would never return coins i.e when we have the amount to be non-negative for one of the coins, see documentation from `Add()` above.

### Recommended Mitigation Steps

Replace direct coin construction with `sdk.NewCoin`:

```diff
-		txFee = txFee.Add(
-			sdk.Coin{
-				Denom:  evm.EVMBankDenom,
-				Amount: sdkmath.NewIntFromBigInt(txData.Fee()),
-			},
-		)
+
+   txFee = txFee.Add(sdk.NewCoin(evm.EVMBankDenom, sdkmath.NewIntFromBigInt(txData.Fee())))
```

This ensures proper validation of:

- Denomination format
- Amount constraints
- Overall coin structure integrity

To put this in retrospect, this is also the exact approach being used by the inspired Ethermint's implementation, see [https://github.com/kshlsa/ethermint/blob/v0.15.0/app/ante/eth.go#L435](https://github.com/kshlsa/ethermint/blob/v0.15.0/app/ante/eth.go#L435).

